  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday's revenue miss is Juneteenth\u2014not execution. Port Washington landed at \u20131.1% versus the DOW-adjusted expectation; Hicksville actually beat it by 9.4%. Juneteenth depressed bookings as expected. What matters is the underlying trend: Port is on pace to grow June double-digit YoY, with appointment volume up 13% MTD; Hicksville is tracking 12% revenue growth and 24% appointment growth MTD, and your team at that location is running hot\u2014$420 per groomer yesterday against a $389 trailing baseline. The real strength here is capacity deployment: you're running 4.2 groomer-days per day at Port (up from 3.8 last month) and 2.2 at Hicksville (up from 1.8), and both stores are monetizing that growth.\n\nOne concern: Hicksville's visit-cadence bucket is drifting. The 12+ week gap cohort grew from 8.4% to 10.2%\u2014a subtle sign customers are stretching their intervals. With only 58 total lapsed customers (vs. Port's 486), this is a smaller pool, but the trajectory warrants attention. At Port, retail is holding steady and add-on attach is actually climbing (62% vs. 59% last week), so upsell discipline is sound there.\n\nInventory reorder is urgent at both locations\u2014you're short on X-Small Treat Tables and Cannoli (top performers) and running negative stock on some items. Port's bather hours yesterday were strong (6.6h), giving you throughput cushion. Hicksville has zero bather coverage, which is artificially constraining groomer capacity.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today you have 4 groomer-days (3 Port, 1 Hicksville) with 8h bather support at Port and zero at Hicksville; tomorrow is a zero-groomer Saturday (holiday bleed); next 7 days you have 39 groomer-days scheduled (30 Port, 9 Hicksville) with 43h bather hours (all Port).**"
